WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.075
Hi, I'm Neymar Aldaner, and this is Module 5 of OS

2
00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:03.760
Work.

3
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.815
In Modules 3 and 4, you set up the training folder

4
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.360
and the config folder, with support files.

5
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.480
Now those folders get instructions the agent follows.

6
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.535
In this model, you write rules, you can check in the

7
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.435
delivery, separate what applies to every project from what applies

8
00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.440
to a single folder, turn a repeated step-by-step into a skill,

9
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.360
keep a short, dated memory, record failures as small protections,

10
00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:28.200
and test what you built with three cases.

11
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.755
We'll keep going with Denise, the Pedagogical Coordinator, and Lucia,

12
00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.055
the 8th grade Science Teacher.

13
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.735
Each lesson has a short practice.

14
00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.615
When I ask, pause the video and do it right away,

15
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.075
on your computer.

16
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.715
Think about the science lab.

17
00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:44.795
On the door, there's a sign that says how to work

18
00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:45.675
inside.

19
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.675
Agents.md does the same for a project folder.

20
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:52.395
It's a simple text file, in Markdown, that you open and

21
00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:54.020
edit in a text editor.

22
00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.915
And Codex looks for that file on its own when it

23
00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.700
starts working in the folder.

24
00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.935
Lúcia already has the training folder, Meu Primeiro Projeto.

25
00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:06.695
Inside it are the README, the Agents.md, the Entradas folder with

26
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.275
a fictional sales spreadsheet, and the Sedas folder, where the agent

27
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.595
delivers the drafts.

28
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:15.695
Agents.md is the first file she's going to improve.

29
00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.620
If you didn't set up that folder, no problem.

30
00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.415
In your personal folder, create projetos, inside it, meu primeiro

31
00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:25.320
projeto, and an empty agent.mark.

32
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.260
What goes in this file?

33
00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:27.920
Four topics.

34
00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:32.535
Where to start reading, how to check the result, what not

35
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.960
to do, and the delivery format.

36
00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:39.380
The school's history and the reasons behind each decision stay out.

37
00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.275
A short file gets read in full.

38
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.775
A long file hides the rule that matters in the middle

39
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:46.380
of paragraphs.

40
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:51.395
Denise opened the Agents.md she had written for the Meeting Minutes

41
00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:52.080
project.

42
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.835
It had two paragraphs about the school's founding and only one

43
00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:56.815
work rule.

44
00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:00.641
She deleted the paragraphs and kept sentences like this.

45
00:02:01.761 --> 00:02:04.036
Read the readme before making changes.

46
00:02:04.061 --> 00:02:07.036
Use only the entradas and saidas folders.

47
00:02:07.061 --> 00:02:09.656
Report what you changed and how you checked it.

48
00:02:09.681 --> 00:02:12.696
Those sentences change what the agent does.

49
00:02:12.721 --> 00:02:16.216
The paragraphs about the founding just took up space.

50
00:02:16.241 --> 00:02:18.336
Now, the sign test.

51
00:02:18.361 --> 00:02:21.236
Wear safety goggles before the experiment is something you can check

52
00:02:21.261 --> 00:02:22.221
by looking.

53
00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:23.996
Be careful, isn't.

54
00:02:24.021 --> 00:02:26.341
The same goes for agents.md.

55
00:02:27.681 --> 00:02:31.536
A good rule asks for an action and leaves evidence you

56
00:02:31.561 --> 00:02:32.341
can see in the delivery.

57
00:02:33.681 --> 00:02:38.716
Lucia replaced be excellent with compare the report total with the

58
00:02:38.741 --> 00:02:40.976
spreadsheet and state the difference.

59
00:02:41.001 --> 00:02:44.316
In the next delivery, the agent wrote the sum and the

60
00:02:44.341 --> 00:02:46.901
difference and she checked it in one minute.

61
00:02:48.741 --> 00:02:50.001
Start with five rules.

62
00:02:50.121 --> 00:02:51.696
like the ones on screen.

63
00:02:51.721 --> 00:02:55.236
If you get stuck, use the test sentence, I'll know it

64
00:02:55.261 --> 00:02:58.996
was followed, because the delivery shows such and such.

65
00:02:59.021 --> 00:03:00.256
Your practice?

66
00:03:00.281 --> 00:03:04.056
Pause the video, open the agents.md in the training folder, and

67
00:03:04.081 --> 00:03:07.916
write the purpose and five rules, each with its evidence next

68
00:03:07.941 --> 00:03:08.321
to it.

69
00:03:08.801 --> 00:03:10.336
It takes about 10 minutes.

70
00:03:10.361 --> 00:03:13.261
The rules from the last lesson apply only in that folder.

71
00:03:13.321 --> 00:03:16.536
What about the ones you want in every project?

72
00:03:16.561 --> 00:03:17.896
Think about school.

73
00:03:17.921 --> 00:03:20.936
The rule book applies to the whole building and the class

74
00:03:20.961 --> 00:03:23.416
agreement applies in the classroom.

75
00:03:23.441 --> 00:03:25.936
Agents.md works the same way.

76
00:03:25.961 --> 00:03:30.296
By default, the global one lives in a hidden folder called .codex

77
00:03:30.321 --> 00:03:32.581
inside your personal folder.

78
00:03:32.721 --> 00:03:35.256
The project one lives in the project folder.

79
00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:38.116
In the terminal, the tilde sign is the shortcut to your

80
00:03:38.141 --> 00:03:39.136
personal folder.

81
00:03:39.161 --> 00:03:42.896
And a folder whose name starts with a dot is hidden.

82
00:03:42.921 --> 00:03:45.816
Denise wants the agent to always say what it checked, in

83
00:03:45.841 --> 00:03:46.936
any project.

84
00:03:46.961 --> 00:03:49.336
That rule went into the global file.

85
00:03:49.361 --> 00:03:52.996
But compare the total with the attendance spreadsheet only makes sense

86
00:03:53.021 --> 00:03:54.081
in the attendance project.

87
00:03:55.141 --> 00:03:58.742
The two work together, and the global one stays small.

88
00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.160
A subfolder can have its own agents.md.

89
00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:07.435
With codecs open in it, both apply and the closest instruction

90
00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:10.080
takes precedence on what it covers.

91
00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:13.100
Whatever it doesn't mention still comes from the file above.

92
00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:18.215
Lucia created a provas subfolder for tests with the rule answer

93
00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:19.935
key in a separate file.

94
00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:21.935
Opened in provas, it applies.

95
00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:24.020
In the exercise lists, it doesn't.

96
00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.860
There's also agents.override column.

97
00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:30.615
In the same folder, Codex reads the override and ignores agent's

98
00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:31.580
dot mark.

99
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.955
Forgotten in a copied folder, it explains a lot of strange

100
00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:35.860
behavior.

101
00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:38.000
And none of these files beats three things.

102
00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:43.155
The system instructions, the permissions, and your explicit request

103
00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:44.000
in the conversation.

104
00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.555
Denise's file says, delivering bullet points, but today she asked

105
00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:50.160
for a continuous paragraph.

106
00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:51.300
Today's request wins.

107
00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:54.440
How do you know which instructions apply in a folder?

108
00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:58.095
Ask Codex to summarize the instructions it loaded and say which

109
00:04:58.120 --> 00:04:59.960
file each one came from.

110
00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:01.760
Then open the files and compare.

111
00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:05.836
Lúcia did that, and the rule report, what you checked, showed

112
00:05:05.861 --> 00:05:07.916
up, which she hadn't written there.

113
00:05:07.941 --> 00:05:11.416
It was in the global file, created in March and forgotten.

114
00:05:11.441 --> 00:05:13.416
Your practice, about 10 minutes.

115
00:05:13.441 --> 00:05:14.496
Pause the video.

116
00:05:14.521 --> 00:05:19.676
In the terminal, go into the training folder and run ls-a,

117
00:05:19.701 --> 00:05:23.636
which also shows hidden files, and write down whether there's an

118
00:05:23.661 --> 00:05:24.641
override.

119
00:05:24.781 --> 00:05:27.876
run cat on the global agents dot mark.

120
00:05:27.901 --> 00:05:30.236
If it says the file doesn't exist, you don't have a

121
00:05:30.261 --> 00:05:30.801
global file.

122
00:05:31.761 --> 00:05:32.956
And that's fine.

123
00:05:32.981 --> 00:05:37.756
Finally, open codecs, ask for the summary saying don't change anything,

124
00:05:37.781 --> 00:05:40.576
and compare each rule with its source file.

125
00:05:40.601 --> 00:05:42.456
A rule says what to respect.

126
00:05:42.481 --> 00:05:46.616
What about the procedure you repeat every week, step by step?

127
00:05:46.641 --> 00:05:48.116
Back to the lab.

128
00:05:48.141 --> 00:05:51.096
The sign on the door applies every day.

129
00:05:51.121 --> 00:05:53.956
The experiment sheet comes out of the binder only on the

130
00:05:53.981 --> 00:05:55.996
day of that experiment.

131
00:05:56.021 --> 00:05:58.576
Agents.md is the sign.

132
00:05:58.601 --> 00:05:59.896
The skill is the sheet.

133
00:05:59.921 --> 00:06:03.936
A skill gathers the instructions for an activity that repeats, and

134
00:06:03.961 --> 00:06:06.903
the agent triggers it when the task calls for it.

135
00:06:07.383 --> 00:06:10.238
Every Friday, Lucia explained to the agent how to put together

136
00:06:10.263 --> 00:06:11.483
the weekly exercise list.

137
00:06:12.823 --> 00:06:16.758
Read that week's lesson, choose five questions, keep the answer keys

138
00:06:16.783 --> 00:06:17.898
separate.

139
00:06:17.923 --> 00:06:22.998
That step-by-step became a skill and agents.md kept its five rules.

140
00:06:23.023 --> 00:06:27.078
Both are right, each in its role.

141
00:06:27.103 --> 00:06:31.338
Separating them avoids loading the whole manual into every task.

142
00:06:31.363 --> 00:06:34.158
A skill lives in a folder with its name, in a

143
00:06:34.183 --> 00:06:36.818
file called skill.md.

144
00:06:36.843 --> 00:06:39.978
At the top between two lines of three dashes come the

145
00:06:40.003 --> 00:06:41.203
name and the description.

146
00:06:42.523 --> 00:06:44.083
That block is the header.

147
00:06:44.963 --> 00:06:48.878
After it comes the procedure, what goes in, the steps, what

148
00:06:48.903 --> 00:06:50.958
comes out, and how to check.

149
00:06:50.983 --> 00:06:54.198
The description is the most important part, because it's what the

150
00:06:54.223 --> 00:06:57.818
agent reads to decide whether to trigger the skill.

151
00:06:57.843 --> 00:07:01.038
It needs to say in which situation to use it and

152
00:07:01.063 --> 00:07:02.343
where the skill stops.

153
00:07:02.483 --> 00:07:06.398
The first description of Denise's report skill was Help with reports.

154
00:07:06.423 --> 00:07:10.599
The agent triggered it even for a meeting minutes request.

155
00:07:10.624 --> 00:07:11.404
She rewrote it.

156
00:07:12.324 --> 00:07:15.479
Generate a weekly report draft when the user provides a sales

157
00:07:15.504 --> 00:07:19.379
CSV and don't use it to send reports or handle credentials,

158
00:07:19.404 --> 00:07:21.219
which are passwords and access keys.

159
00:07:21.244 --> 00:07:22.519
Where do you keep it?

160
00:07:22.544 --> 00:07:29.679
A project skill goes in .agents slash skills inside the project folder.

161
00:07:29.704 --> 00:07:32.939
A personal skill, one you want in every project goes in

162
00:07:32.964 --> 00:07:35.019
that same folder but inside your personal folder.

163
00:07:35.044 --> 00:07:39.259
The .codex folder belongs to codex, with the configuration and the

164
00:07:39.284 --> 00:07:40.499
global file.

165
00:07:40.524 --> 00:07:42.004
Skills don't go there.

166
00:07:42.084 --> 00:07:46.759
Denise kept the meeting minutes skill in the minutes project and

167
00:07:46.784 --> 00:07:50.319
the spelling review one, which she uses for everything, in her

168
00:07:50.344 --> 00:07:51.099
personal folder.

169
00:07:51.124 --> 00:07:54.439
Your practice, about 10 minutes, pause the video.

170
00:07:54.464 --> 00:07:58.259
Copy the template from the lesson and save it as skill.md

171
00:07:58.284 --> 00:08:00.779
in the training folder with with the file type set to

172
00:08:00.804 --> 00:08:01.539
all files.

173
00:08:01.564 --> 00:08:06.479
In the terminal, create the Relatorial Semanal folder with mark dir-p,

174
00:08:06.504 --> 00:08:11.419
move the file into it with mv and run head-4.

175
00:08:11.444 --> 00:08:13.784
You're done when the four header lines appear.

176
00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:17.080
A skill doesn't remember what you agreed on yesterday.

177
00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:20.380
So where do you keep what needs to stay valid?

178
00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:22.400
First, a clarification.

179
00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:25.220
The model doesn't change because you talked to it.

180
00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.915
The operational memory in this course is a set of files

181
00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:30.040
the agent reads when you point to them.

182
00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:31.940
It works under two conditions.

183
00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:35.775
The agent reads the part that matters, and someone keeps the

184
00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:37.095
file up to date.

185
00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:41.055
In module 4, Denise created the config folder next to the

186
00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:41.940
projects.

187
00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:47.335
That's where Memoria lives, with stable facts and preferences, decisos,

188
00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.795
with the decisions made and the reason, and falhas, with the

189
00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:53.395
causes of failures, which is the topic of the next lesson.

190
00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:57.275
If you skip that part, create the config folder inside Progettos

191
00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:03.155
and init a file called memoria.md.

192
00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:05.060
What goes into memory?

193
00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:06.195
Three things.

194
00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:10.480
Stable facts, decisions, and causes of failures.

195
00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:13.515
every sentence from every conversation.

196
00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:17.438
Copying the whole history adds volume and can bring back an

197
00:09:17.463 --> 00:09:18.638
old instruction.

198
00:09:18.663 --> 00:09:20.483
And nobody can check a huge file.

199
00:09:20.863 --> 00:09:22.303
Look at Lucia's case.

200
00:09:22.343 --> 00:09:26.058
She had 200 lines of conversation from March to September, and

201
00:09:26.083 --> 00:09:29.038
in the middle, a sentence asking for the old list format,

202
00:09:29.063 --> 00:09:30.398
which had already changed.

203
00:09:30.423 --> 00:09:32.103
She replaced all of it with three lines.

204
00:09:32.743 --> 00:09:36.118
The review date, eighth grade materials with accessible language and

205
00:09:36.143 --> 00:09:40.058
fictional examples, and a weekly list with five questions and a

206
00:09:40.083 --> 00:09:41.298
separate answer key.

207
00:09:41.323 --> 00:09:42.983
From 200 lines to three.

208
00:09:44.083 --> 00:09:45.423
And no outdated instructions.

209
00:09:46.203 --> 00:09:49.038
In the next task, she pointed to that file instead of

210
00:09:49.063 --> 00:09:50.183
repeating the conversation.

211
00:09:50.723 --> 00:09:53.338
The file being in the folder doesn't guarantee the agent will

212
00:09:53.363 --> 00:09:53.678
read it.

213
00:09:53.703 --> 00:09:56.918
Say in your request which file to check and ask it

214
00:09:56.943 --> 00:09:58.683
to cite the fact it used.

215
00:09:58.823 --> 00:10:02.198
Denise asked for a three-line reminder about the parent meeting, pointing

216
00:10:02.223 --> 00:10:02.798
to the memory.

217
00:10:02.823 --> 00:10:05.478
The answer ended with the fact it used.

218
00:10:05.503 --> 00:10:09.583
Reminders to families of up to three lines, with no abbreviations.

219
00:10:09.743 --> 00:10:11.678
She checked it in the file.

220
00:10:11.703 --> 00:10:13.618
And stable facts change too.

221
00:10:13.643 --> 00:10:16.700
Mark the review date at the top and note the source

222
00:10:16.725 --> 00:10:17.640
of each fact.

223
00:10:17.665 --> 00:10:22.420
In September, Denise deleted reminders go home printed in the backpack

224
00:10:22.445 --> 00:10:25.440
because the school switched to the app.

225
00:10:25.465 --> 00:10:26.720
Your practice.

226
00:10:26.745 --> 00:10:30.440
Pause, write the date and three facts with their source.

227
00:10:30.465 --> 00:10:32.880
No student names and no passwords.

228
00:10:32.905 --> 00:10:36.680
Then, in codex or your chat, ask for a short task

229
00:10:36.705 --> 00:10:39.980
pointing to the file and check the fact it cites.

230
00:10:40.005 --> 00:10:43.320
When something goes wrong, what goes into the record?

231
00:10:43.345 --> 00:10:45.360
Start by separating two things.

232
00:10:45.385 --> 00:10:48.420
The report came out empty is the symptom, what you saw.

233
00:10:48.445 --> 00:10:52.580
The cause is the reason you observed, like the spreadsheet had

234
00:10:52.605 --> 00:10:53.820
no records.

235
00:10:53.845 --> 00:10:56.640
Notice, observed, not assumed.

236
00:10:56.665 --> 00:10:59.960
In the training project, Lucia saw the report come out empty.

237
00:10:59.985 --> 00:11:03.220
Before touching anything, she wrote down the symptom and opened the

238
00:11:03.245 --> 00:11:04.140
input spreadsheet.

239
00:11:04.165 --> 00:11:05.800
It had no records at all.

240
00:11:05.825 --> 00:11:09.345
After the symptom and the cause come two more columns.

241
00:11:10.765 --> 00:11:13.940
The smallest fix, which here is checking the number of rows

242
00:11:13.965 --> 00:11:18.423
before generating, and the verification, which says how you know the

243
00:11:18.448 --> 00:11:19.123
fix works.

244
00:11:19.148 --> 00:11:22.823
An empty spreadsheet produces a warning, not a report.

245
00:11:22.848 --> 00:11:26.503
All in a single table row, in the file has file,

246
00:11:26.528 --> 00:11:28.303
with the date and the type.

247
00:11:28.328 --> 00:11:29.903
And what type of failure is it?

248
00:11:29.928 --> 00:11:33.663
A request failure is when the goal was ambiguous, or information

249
00:11:33.688 --> 00:11:34.303
was missing.

250
00:11:34.328 --> 00:11:38.283
An infrastructure failure is when the text was fine, but something

251
00:11:38.308 --> 00:11:43.063
outside it failed, a missing file or a process that stopped.

252
00:11:43.088 --> 00:11:45.343
Denise had one of each in the same week.

253
00:11:45.368 --> 00:11:49.503
The minutes summary came out 2 pages long, because the request

254
00:11:49.528 --> 00:11:50.583
didn't say the length.

255
00:11:50.608 --> 00:11:53.823
The fix was in the text, up to 10 lines.

256
00:11:53.848 --> 00:11:57.983
The attendance report didn't come out because the spreadsheet wasn't

257
00:11:58.008 --> 00:11:58.583
in the folder.

258
00:11:58.608 --> 00:12:01.048
The fix was a check.

259
00:12:01.228 --> 00:12:04.283
Confirm the file exists before starting.

260
00:12:04.308 --> 00:12:06.808
When in doubt, ask.

261
00:12:07.168 --> 00:12:09.583
If I had written it better, would it have worked?

262
00:12:09.608 --> 00:12:11.563
If yes, it was the request.

263
00:12:11.588 --> 00:12:14.448
If it was both, mark both.

264
00:12:15.428 --> 00:12:18.268
And prefer the smallest fix to a rebuild.

265
00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:22.200
Faced with the empty report, Lucia thought about switching models.

266
00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:23.340
That would take two hours.

267
00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:27.020
And the empty spreadsheet would keep breaking the next report.

268
00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:28.875
One new line was enough.

269
00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:35.015
And that line goes into agents.md, or the skill, which the

270
00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:36.740
agent reads on every task.

271
00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:41.115
Denise wrote in the attendance one, Before reading, check that the

272
00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:42.120
spreadsheet exists.

273
00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:45.655
If it's missing, stop and say which file is missing.

274
00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:48.800
The following week, the agent stopped and warned her.

275
00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:51.740
Your practice, about 12 minutes, pause.

276
00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:55.275
In the terminal, list the Entradas folder and look for an

277
00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:57.500
October file that doesn't exist.

278
00:12:57.580 --> 00:13:01.615
Record that fictional failure in Falhas, paste the ruling to agents.md,

279
00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:04.300
open codex, and ask for the October report.

280
00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:08.235
Check that it stops, says which file is missing, and doesn't

281
00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:08.920
make up numbers.

282
00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:11.455
The skill worked once, does it always work?

283
00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:15.060
To find out, you'll test it with three cases.

284
00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:18.835
And before running it, you write on a checklist what each

285
00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:20.540
case should produce.

286
00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:23.132
That way you compare with a reference, not with your impression

287
00:13:23.157 --> 00:13:24.272
in the moment.

288
00:13:24.297 --> 00:13:26.972
Denise set up the checklist the way she sets up the

289
00:13:26.997 --> 00:13:28.612
evacuation drill sheet.

290
00:13:28.637 --> 00:13:32.052
First, what should happen, then the run-through.

291
00:13:32.077 --> 00:13:35.572
In the normal case, the spreadsheet has a notebook at 3550,

292
00:13:35.597 --> 00:13:39.172
a pen at 950 and a planner at 55 and the

293
00:13:39.197 --> 00:13:40.672
expected total is 100.

294
00:13:40.697 --> 00:13:43.912
In the incomplete case, the pen has no value, so the

295
00:13:43.937 --> 00:13:47.552
expected result is the known total of 9050 and a pending

296
00:13:47.577 --> 00:13:48.432
IT for the PEN.

297
00:13:48.457 --> 00:13:52.112
In the not as agreed case, the request is generate and

298
00:13:52.137 --> 00:13:55.792
send to management, and the expected result is only the draft,

299
00:13:55.817 --> 00:13:57.372
with nothing sent.

300
00:13:57.397 --> 00:14:01.052
In the incomplete case, the right result doesn't invent the missing

301
00:14:01.077 --> 00:14:01.512
value.

302
00:14:01.537 --> 00:14:05.412
It shows the total of what's known and keeps the pending

303
00:14:05.437 --> 00:14:06.952
item in plain view.

304
00:14:06.977 --> 00:14:10.212
The first version of Luce's skill was missing the rule, don't

305
00:14:10.237 --> 00:14:12.192
invent missing values.

306
00:14:12.217 --> 00:14:15.932
And the agent answered with a total of 100, as if

307
00:14:15.957 --> 00:14:17.152
the PEN had a value.

308
00:14:17.177 --> 00:14:20.052
It was the test that reviewed this.

309
00:14:20.077 --> 00:14:23.812
After the fix, the answer brought the source, the draft in

310
00:14:23.837 --> 00:14:28.350
the Sayadas folder, the known total of 9050, and the pen

311
00:14:28.375 --> 00:14:31.090
as a pending item, left blank.

312
00:14:31.115 --> 00:14:35.270
In the third case, Denise deliberately asks to send the report

313
00:14:35.295 --> 00:14:36.370
to management.

314
00:14:36.395 --> 00:14:38.090
Codex can't even send email there.

315
00:14:38.115 --> 00:14:40.730
The test checks whether it respects the limit.

316
00:14:40.755 --> 00:14:44.450
It made the draft, checked the total, and said it didn't

317
00:14:44.475 --> 00:14:47.815
send because the skill stops before sending or publishing.

318
00:14:48.155 --> 00:14:51.015
Looking for the skill's name in the answer isn't a test.

319
00:14:51.095 --> 00:14:55.030
A test means answering three questions, looking at the answer and

320
00:14:55.055 --> 00:14:56.715
the report file in the CIDAS folder.

321
00:14:57.475 --> 00:14:59.275
Does the total equal the sum?

322
00:14:59.495 --> 00:15:02.350
Is the pending item visible, with no made-up number?

323
00:15:02.375 --> 00:15:04.035
Did it stop before sending?

324
00:15:04.135 --> 00:15:07.210
If a case fails, add to the skill the smallest rule

325
00:15:07.235 --> 00:15:09.495
that fixes that failure and run it again.

326
00:15:11.015 --> 00:15:13.035
Lucia didn't rewrite the skill.

327
00:15:13.215 --> 00:15:14.735
She added one line.

328
00:15:14.835 --> 00:15:17.310
An empty value becomes a pending item.

329
00:15:17.335 --> 00:15:18.790
Never fill it in.

330
00:15:18.815 --> 00:15:20.250
And all three cases passed.

331
00:15:20.275 --> 00:15:22.855
Your practice, about 12 minutes.

332
00:15:23.835 --> 00:15:25.370
Pause the video.

333
00:15:25.395 --> 00:15:29.270
Create the two fictional spreadsheets with the commands from the lesson.

334
00:15:29.295 --> 00:15:34.427
For each case, open codex, send the request, exit, and read

335
00:15:34.452 --> 00:15:35.827
the report.

336
00:15:35.852 --> 00:15:39.027
Mark passed or failed on the checklist.

337
00:15:39.052 --> 00:15:42.687
If one fails, add only the smallest rule.

338
00:15:42.712 --> 00:15:44.407
Let's put it all together.

339
00:15:44.432 --> 00:15:48.067
You write instructions, you can check in the delivery with action

340
00:15:48.092 --> 00:15:51.867
and evidence, and you remove from the file whatever doesn't change

341
00:15:51.892 --> 00:15:52.687
the work.

342
00:15:52.712 --> 00:15:55.107
You keep the global file small and the details in the

343
00:15:55.132 --> 00:15:58.387
project knowing that the closest file decides there and that your

344
00:15:58.412 --> 00:16:00.347
explicit request counts more.

345
00:16:00.372 --> 00:16:05.147
You turn the step-by-step that repeats into a skill with a

346
00:16:05.172 --> 00:16:08.707
description that says when to use it and when not to.

347
00:16:08.732 --> 00:16:12.687
You keep in memory only what stays valid with date and

348
00:16:12.712 --> 00:16:16.307
source and you point to the file in your request.

349
00:16:16.332 --> 00:16:20.227
You record failures as small protections where the next run reads

350
00:16:20.252 --> 00:16:21.967
them.

351
00:16:21.992 --> 00:16:25.632
And you test the skill with 3 cases before trusting it.

352
00:16:25.872 --> 00:16:29.187
When you have about 30 minutes, do the module lab in

353
00:16:29.212 --> 00:16:31.547
the supplementary material for lesson 30.

354
00:16:31.572 --> 00:16:35.347
In module 6, Git and GitHub without losing work.

355
00:16:35.372 --> 00:16:36.364
See you there.
